    {
      "id": "patikti",
      "number": "68",
      "heading": "Liking: Patikti with the Dative",
      "statement": "Lithuanian does not say \"I like the book\" but **the book pleases to me**. The verb is **patikti**, the person who likes stands in the **dative**, and the thing liked is the **subject**, in the nominative. It follows that the verb agrees with the thing, not with the person — and that a plural thing gives a plural verb though the person is one.",
      "paradigms": [
        {
          "caption": "The construction compared",
          "columns": [
            "Lithuanian",
            "literally",
            "English"
          ],
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "singular thing",
              "cells": [
                "Man patinka knyga.",
                "to-me pleases book",
                "I like the book."
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "plural thing",
              "cells": [
                "Man patinka knygos.",
                "to-me please books",
                "I like the books."
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "another person",
              "cells": [
                "Jam patinka kava.",
                "to-him pleases coffee",
                "He likes coffee."
              ]
            }
          ],
          "footnote": "Compare *mėgti* (¶39), which is built as English builds it: *aš mėgstu knygą*, with the liker as subject and the thing in the accusative. Both are correct; *patikti* is the commoner in speech."
        }
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Man patinka Vilnius.",
          "gloss": "I like Vilnius."
        },
        {
          "target": "Ar tau patinka kava?",
          "gloss": "Do you like coffee?"
        },
        {
          "target": "Man nepatinka miestas.",
          "gloss": "I do not like the city."
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "dative-pronouns",
        "wanting-liking"
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "reikia",
      "number": "69",
      "heading": "Needing: Reikia with the Dative",
      "statement": "**Reikia** — it is necessary — is **impersonal**: it has no subject at all and never changes its form. The person who needs stands in the **dative**, and what is needed follows either in the **genitive** or as an **infinitive**. English turns the person into a subject and Lithuanian does not.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Man reikia pinigų.",
          "gloss": "I need money.",
          "note": "literally: to-me is-necessary of-money"
        },
        {
          "target": "Man reikia dirbti.",
          "gloss": "I need to work.",
          "note": "infinitive instead of a noun"
        },
        {
          "target": "Jai reikia kavos.",
          "gloss": "She needs coffee."
        },
        {
          "target": "Mums nereikia nieko.",
          "gloss": "We need nothing."
        }
      ],
      "footnotes": [
        "*reikia* never takes an ending for person or number: *man reikia*, *jiems reikia*, alike."
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "dative-pronouns",
        "genitive-quantity",
        "complementary-infinitive"
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "other-dative-verbs",
      "number": "70",
      "heading": "Other Verbs Taking the Dative",
      "statement": "A group of everyday verbs governs the dative where English would use a direct object or a preposition. **Tikti**, to suit; **skambinti**, to telephone; **padėti**, to help. With these the dative is not an indirect object but the only object there is, and using an accusative is simply wrong.",
      "paradigms": [
        {
          "caption": "Verbs with the dative",
          "columns": [
            "Lithuanian",
            "English"
          ],
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "tikti",
              "cells": [
                "Man netinka kava.",
                "Coffee does not suit me."
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "skambinti",
              "cells": [
                "Skambinu draugui.",
                "I am telephoning a friend."
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "padėti",
              "cells": [
                "Ji padeda mamai.",
                "She is helping her mother."
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Aš padedu broliui.",
          "gloss": "I am helping my brother.",
          "note": "not *padedu brolį*"
        },
        {
          "target": "Ar tau tinka šis laikas?",
          "gloss": "Does this time suit you?"
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "dative-endings",
        "patikti"
      ],
      "core": true
    }
  ],
